  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nRest of the Weekend: Anomalous Warmth Continues\n\nStrong southerly Gulf flow coupled with a +2 to +3 standard \ndeviation low to mid tropospheric airmass--ranking near the \nwarmest of all time in the NAEFS and ENS climatology--sets the \nstage for near to record breaking warm temperatures this \nweekend. Highs today look to remain on track to reach the \nmid-80s with a few locales in the valleys possibly reaching into\nthe upper 80s (a 10-20% chance). This will be very close to, if\nnot exceeding, the record high temperatures set for today. The \nperiphery of a 40 to 50 kt LLJ starts to shift into the region \nthrough the day, resulting in afternoon surface gusts of 30-35 \nkts, mainly west of the Mississippi River. The potential for 40-\nkt gusts in this same corridor drops off quickly to around \n10-20%, but forecast soundings from the HRRR do hint at that \npossibility as we head later into the afternoon. \n\nThis aforementioned LLJ slides further east overnight, with \nforecast profiles showing winds of 40 to 50 kts only 1-2 kft \nabove the surface. These winds will slow the stabilizing of the \nboundary layer and keep winds at higher elevations gusting to \n20-30kts for much of the night. Lows in areas that remain more \nmixed look to only fall into the low to mid 60s--i.e. the \naverage high for this time of year. Meanwhile, decoupled locales\nin sheltered or valley areas will see lows in the 50s.\n\nUpper tropospheric ridging builds northward into the north \ncentral CONUS for Sunday, tempering winds but still keeping the \nwarmth in place. The deterministic guidance has wavered a bit on\nwhether high temperatures will eclipse the records for the day,\nbut no matter how you slice the forecast, it will still be a \nvery warm day. \n\n\nMonday: Front Arrives - Light Shower Potential\n\nRecent trends in the short and medium range guidance have been \nfor a later arrival of a cold front that brings an end to our \nheat. The net result is that the heat looks to linger through \nthe day on Monday with showers staying primarily north of I-90 \nlater in the afternoon and overnight. Any rainfall looks to be \nlight with the probability of seeing 0.10\" situated around \n20-40% and a <10% chance of seeing 0.25\". The front and the \naccompanying showers are on track to depart by sunrise Tuesday.\n\n\nRest of the Week: Seasonal Temps, Rain Risk Returns Mid to Late\nWeek\n\nConfidence in the forecast wanes beyond Wednesday as the medium\nto longer range solutions diverge quickly on the evolution of \nan upstream Great Basin cutoff low. One scenario, led by the EPS\nmembership, fails to absorb this low with the next northern \nstream wave passage, keeping the subsequent surge of polar air \nnorth of the region. The GEFS, meanwhile, has the reverse \nsolution that opens the door for some of the coldest air of the \nseason to arrive by the end of the week with highs potentially \nin the 30s and 40s (versus 50s and 60s in the EPS). The AI \nguidance for both the EC/GEFS are favoring the colder solutions,\nso these will bear watching for the potential for freeze \nwarnings for next weekend. To be fair, the entire region has \nalready passed its average first freeze (32 degrees) date, so \nany remaining vegetation is on borrowed time.\n\nA scenario in which the cutoff low ejects from the Great Basin \nwould promote a strong warm sector response/theta-e advection \ninto the central CONUS late in the week, with rainfall \nprobabilities for 0.50\" already around 30-50% and outlier \nsolutions (10-20%) exceeding 1\" for 24 hour rainfall amounts.",
